# Model Split for App-Code-vs-API-Doc Investigations

When investigating Make custom (SDK) app code against vendor/API documentation
(e.g. checking whether a module/RPC/function correctly reflects the vendor's
API — required fields, endpoints, schemas, deprecations, etc.), default to this
split for price/performance:

## Orchestrator (main thread — Claude Sonnet 5 thinking-high)

- Plans the investigation.
- Reads/fetches Make app code directly via MCP (`custom-apps_*`, `rpc_execute`, etc.).
- Performs the actual comparison/judgment between code and documentation.
- Writes the final verified conclusion with citations.
- Never blindly trusts a subagent's claim for anything load-bearing to the
  conclusion — re-verifies against what it can see directly when it matters.

## Subagents (`gpt-5.6-sol-medium`) for narrow, cheap legwork

Delegate to a `Task` subagent with `model: "gpt-5.6-sol-medium"` for steps that
are I/O-heavy but low-reasoning, e.g.:

- Fetching/summarizing a vendor's API reference page for a specific endpoint
  or object type.
- Listing all RPCs/functions/modules in an app before diving into specifics.
- Pulling raw code for a specific function via MCP and returning it verbatim.
- A first-pass diff between two versions of a module.

## Notes

- This is the default; don't ask before spinning up a Sol subagent for this
  kind of legwork on these tasks.
- If a task turns out to need more rigor than expected (ambiguous vendor docs,
  conflicting sources, high-stakes conclusion), pull that step back into the
  Sonnet orchestrator instead of trusting the Sol subagent's output as-is.
- This split applies specifically to Make SDK app code vs. API documentation
  investigations — not a general rule for all subagent delegation.
